// Local development config (per ADR 0014).
//
// TODO (Phase 3, post-Twilio): create `Staging.xcconfig` alongside this file
// with the hosted spur-staging Supabase URL + publishable key + a real
// LAN/server URL, then point the Release configuration at it (Xcode →
// project → Info → Configurations → Release → Spur → Staging). Until that
// lands, Release falls back to Local so simulator Release builds still work.
//
// Anon (publishable) key is committed deliberately — Supabase markets it as
// client-safe. Service-role key never appears in any xcconfig.
//
// `//` is the xcconfig comment marker, so URLs use the `$()` escape between
// the colon and the double-slash to keep the parser from eating the rest of
// the line.

SUPABASE_URL = http:/$()/127.0.0.1:54321
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY = sb_publishable_ACJWlzQHlZjBrEguHvfOxg_3BJgxAaH
SERVER_URL = http:/$()/127.0.0.1:8080

// Spur-Info.plist references the three values above as $(SUPABASE_URL),
// $(SUPABASE_ANON_KEY), $(SERVER_URL) — Xcode expands them at build time.
// (INFOPLIST_KEY_* prefix is rejected by Xcode for non-Apple-known keys, so
// custom keys must live in a real plist file.) Spur-Info.plist also carries
// the NSAppTransportSecurity exception, which is dict-shaped and can't be
// expressed in xcconfig at all.
INFOPLIST_FILE = Spur-Info.plist

// iOS Simulator reaches the host via 127.0.0.1. A real device on the same
// wifi substitutes the host's LAN IP here, but device runs are gated on the
// paid Apple Developer account (see ios/README.md §Signing).
